WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.920
So who's my guest this week?

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.293
It's the former senior White
House advisor, Fiona Hill.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.370
It was one of the most
compelling appearances

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.600
during the Trump impeachment hearings.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.360
Blunt, composed and incredibly distinctive

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.980
with her strong Northern English accent.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:17.890
President Trump's top Russia expert

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:21.570
was devastating in her
assessment of policy and culture

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.270
in Trump's White House.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:26.040
Her warning about Putin's
danger to the West

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.970
and how American ideals that
were so appealing to her

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:30.610
were under threat.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.440
Putin is always looking out to see

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.810
if there is any hint that
we will not follow through

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:37.080
on promises that we have made,

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.550
because he will always
follow through on a threat.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:43.320
Fiona Hill's journey from
a poor English mining town

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.920
to working for three
presidents in Washington

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.620
is documented in her book,

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.510
There Is Nothing For You Here.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.480
Where she takes her story of
social mobility and opportunity

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.710
and argues that class and racial barriers

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.250
are still holding too many back.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.170
Here's a bit of what's coming up.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:01.426
And imagine if Trump

00:01:01.426 --> 00:01:03.890
had actually succeeded on January 6th

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:05.950
and the members of the
January 6th committee

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.500
in the US Congress are pretty convinced

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.470
that we were very close to him succeeding,

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.543
had not Vice President Pence

00:01:12.543 --> 00:01:17.543
basically refused to block the
transfer of executive power.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.370
I mean, we'd be in a
totally different place.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.310
I mean, Vladimir Putin
would've probably just

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.133
driven right into Ukraine himself.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.730
Fiona, thank you so much
for coming to talk to us.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.150
Thanks, Emma glad to be with you.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:41.720
Really interested to hear
what you have to say,

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.240
especially because for a lot of people,

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:44.290
they wouldn't have heard of you

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.700
before that first impeachment inquiry

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.160
in which you appeared,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.770
and I wonder for you, do
you replay that moment?

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:53.250
Do you think about it?

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:54.440
And what do you think about?

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:55.870
I actually don't.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.980
I mean, maybe that sounds
a little bit strange,

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.830
but it was kind of...

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:00.800
It's one of those things

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.895
where you're so focused in the moment,

00:02:02.895 --> 00:02:04.500
that when you come out again,

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.200
it's almost like it didn't happen

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.380
because it was such an intense
period of concentration,

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.370
a lot of effort leading up to it.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.710
And then, you know, the kind of aftermath

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:15.610
was sort of moving onto the next thing,

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.600
because so much happened
as a consequence of that

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:18.950
that I hadn't anticipated

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.800
that I find it very kind
of hard looking back to it.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.210
I find it fascinating in your book,

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.840
you talk out this being
prepared for the room,

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.670
that it was gonna be quite cold,

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.820
and also how you looked as a woman.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.130
All these things had to be factored in

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:35.120
when I'm sure you just wanted
to give the information.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:35.953
Exactly.

00:02:35.953 --> 00:02:37.120
I mean, I didn't know I'd
have to think about this.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.080
I mean, literally one of
the very first questions was

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.270
we're gonna have to figure
out what you're going to wear.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:42.548
I thought, what?

00:02:42.548 --> 00:02:45.068
And I said, well, does that happen to men?

00:02:45.068 --> 00:02:45.910
They said not so much.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.830
But the point is that
as a woman, you know,

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:49.660
people are already wondering who you are

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.460
and they literally said to me,

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.220
you don't want to be a
distraction from the truth.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:55.930
And the reason I wanted
to put this in the book

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:58.360
is that most times when men write a book,

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:00.680
nobody asks them about
how it is to be a man.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.990
Nobody even kind of thinks about

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:03.630
what it is that they were wearing

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.570
or the particular setting.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:09.240
And they just go there
and they do their job.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:10.830
I mean, yes, if you're
gonna appear on television,

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.480
everybody now these days
with high definition TV

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.280
has to have some makeup
on and their head done,

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:18.460
because otherwise they
look like they've died,

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.510
kind of become just the
whole bright lights effect.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.641
But for something as serious

00:03:23.641 --> 00:03:25.490
and consequential as a testimony,

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.170
I thought the whole fact that

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:29.290
there was focus on how I would look

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:31.570
as part of my presenting myself

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:33.990
so that people would listen to my answers

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:35.800
and just for telling the truth,

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:36.950
I mean, that really hit me

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:37.920
and thought about, you know,

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.600
what a difference it
really is to be a woman

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.230
in these kinds of settings.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:42.370
And what did you do?

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.280
How did you make sure you were listened to

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.380
in terms of how you looked

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:46.420
and then how did you feel?

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:47.500
Did you have to wear extra layers?

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:48.790
Did you have to take the advice?

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:51.700
I did and I had a really good PR person,

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.110
a woman that I've mentioned in the book

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:54.260
called Molly Levinson,

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.490
who did this for a living and had been in

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.030
the broadcast media as well as a producer,

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.040
but who had worked with people

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.760
in these kinds of settings

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.390
for a long period of time.

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:06.230
And she literally went
through my wardrobe.

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:09.640
She said, you know, you have
to look a particular look.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:10.850
She said that we'll be picked up

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:13.230
by the Washington Post style section.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:14.660
Well, that look was,

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.040
when we were picked up by the
Washington Post style section,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.890
which I was pretty incredulous
about at the beginning,

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:21.710
they said we were reassuringly dull.

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:22.940
So that was what she was looking for,

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:25.810
because I was basically
testifying with another person,

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:28.930
David Holmes, from the US embassy in Kiev.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.305
Did he look reassuringly dull?

00:04:30.305 --> 00:04:31.971
Well, I thought he looked
quite smart, actually.

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:33.680
But the whole view was, you know,

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:35.780
reassuringly dull bureaucrat look.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:38.350
There was a Fiona Hill fan club

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.180
that sort of sprung up online

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.820
actually from across the
spectrum in some ways

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:45.250
as people heard what you
had to say and stayed,

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:46.083
a lot of people talking about

00:04:46.083 --> 00:04:47.970
how focused you stayed on the facts.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:49.250
And some of those almost

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:51.350
we'll get to through your expertise,

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:54.930
but did you feel it was
a good use of your time

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:56.180
or do you feel it was
a waste of your time?

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:58.180
Because ultimately it didn't work.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:59.330
I had John Bolton on,

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:02.230
the former National Security
Advisor to Trump very recently

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:03.410
and he didn't testify

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:05.610
because he thought it
would be a waste of time.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.860
It depends on what your
perspective is about

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.970
waste of time or good use of time.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:11.350
In the United States,

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:13.900
there is still this
concept of civics education

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:17.240
where people fully understand
how their government works

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:18.570
and especially important

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:20.790
in something like a
representative democracy,

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:24.240
liberal democracy where
you as the regular person

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.660
wants to feel that your public servants,

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:28.610
your members of Congress,
your members of Parliament

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:31.700
are actually taking your
interest into consideration

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:33.460
and listening to you.

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:36.010
And understanding that there
are checks and balances

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:36.910
on executive power,

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:38.420
particularly on abuses of power,

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.180
which this was clearly the case

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.550
and this whole definition
of crimes and misdemeanors

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:44.370
and how does this process play out?

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:46.040
It was an exercise in liberal democracy.

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.430
It's the checks and the balance,
the oversight of Congress.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:50.030
But ultimately it didn't work.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:53.530
Yeah, but it showed people
that the system was there,

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:55.940
and that everybody got to hear the truth.

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:57.740
And I think a lot of
people came away from that

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:59.580
realizing that it was wrong

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.700
for basically Donald Trump
to ask Volodymyr Zelenskyy,

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.610
the president of Ukraine
for a personal favor.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:08.090
Now, the political outcome of this,

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:10.020
the partisan political outcome of this

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.400
was of course that there was
no censure even of Trump.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.120
He didn't get any kind of penalty,

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.060
just it wasn't even a wrap
on the knuckles or the wrist

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:20.445
from any perspective.

00:06:20.445 --> 00:06:22.699
And as Ambassador
Bolton, John Bolton said,

00:06:22.699 --> 00:06:24.760
well, look, it seems
to be a waste of time,

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:26.560
if anybody was really expecting

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.500
there to be some massive
outcome for Trump himself,

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.060
I think he'd actually also emboldened him

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.540
to go onto the next thing that
he got impeached twice for.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.610
Were there any negative outcomes

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:39.990
for you being out there and
effectively going viral?

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:40.823
Oh, there was a lot.

00:06:40.823 --> 00:06:41.990
I mean, there was all kinds of misogyny

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:43.870
and sexism and threats

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:45.760
and threats of bodily harm

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:47.100
and kind of all kinds of--

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:48.370
How did they come to you?

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:49.510
Mostly through the internet.

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:51.220
So I just stopped engaging.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:53.070
I mean, my sister who
was constantly monitoring

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:56.500
Twitter and Facebook and
things would give me warnings

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:58.660
and she would get in
touch with the platforms

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:00.490
to try to get people to take down

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:02.220
some of the most outrageous things.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:05.340
I'd get phone calls, letters, emails.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:06.400
But actually, I have to say

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.460
that it was a smaller percentage

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:12.230
than what was really an overwhelming,

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:13.410
I would say a positive response

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:16.160
in terms of just thank
you for telling the truth.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:18.670
And again, everybody can tell the truth.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:20.680
So, I didn't think it
was that extraordinary.

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:24.350
And again, there was a lot of
other people who did testify,

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:27.878
my colleagues, I mean the
people have got to know as well.

00:07:27.878 --> 00:07:30.050
I really took away from that

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.500
that there was a kind of a great thirst

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:33.690
for people just standing up there

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:35.800
and telling things like they are.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:37.350
Talking about what you know,

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:39.620
and your expertise, of course, it's Russia

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:42.360
and it's hugely in demand right now,

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:44.370
and I wanted to talk about Ukraine

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:46.890
from the perspective of
all you have looked at

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:49.130
and all you're looking at right now.

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:52.930
Do you think the conflict,
the war was inevitable?

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:53.810
I don't actually.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:54.760
I mean, I think there's all kinds

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:57.100
of junctures along the way in everything,

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.660
where things can take a different path.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:01.170
I think that what happened

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.380
in terms of the decision
making of Vladimir Putin

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:04.460
is he thought, you know,

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:05.900
there was a number of things going on.

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:07.610
First of all, he thought
the west had lost the plot,

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:10.530
that we'd become very weak and distracted,

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:11.860
that indeed all we're doing

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:14.940
is engaging with partisan
fights with each other,

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:16.880
identity politics, you name it,

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.822
but he saw over a long period of time,

00:08:19.822 --> 00:08:23.740
an inability for the west to
stand up to it's own values

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:24.670
and it's own principles,

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:27.360
particularly when it came to
pressure on other countries.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.744
So Ukraine was in the cross hairs.

00:08:29.744 --> 00:08:32.790
Going back to in fact the early 2000s

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:34.600
and even back into the 1990s

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:35.750
and we didn't really do anything.

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.970
So when Georgia was invaded back in 2008,

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:40.760
each time we'd find an excuse

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.168
to not really take very strong measures.

00:08:43.168 --> 00:08:46.780
I also think that two years
of COVID had an impact.

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:49.450
Like the rest of us, he
was kind of sitting down,

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:50.955
you know, hunkered down,

00:08:50.955 --> 00:08:54.740
excluding people from coming close to him,

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:56.440
not wanting to get sick,

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:58.800
but he also became more and more isolated.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:01.670
We could see from the people
who used to get access to him.

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:04.540
So he starts to be basically shrunk down

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:06.030
in his terms of advice

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:08.150
to a smaller and smaller group of people,

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:10.940
and clearly he convinced himself

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:11.773
that he could get away with this

00:09:11.773 --> 00:09:14.060
and that there would be no backlash

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:16.100
either in Ukraine or
anywhere else in the west.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:17.550
But do you think, I mean, for instance,

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:19.230
you've mentioned a whole series of events

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:20.640
leading up to this,

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.510
but for instance, there
are some now saying

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:25.050
that the presidency of Trump

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:27.830
and the attack on January
the sixth on the capital

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:30.560
was a moment, a particular moment.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.710
Did you buy into that?

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:34.090
From Putin's point of view?

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:34.923
Exactly.

00:09:34.923 --> 00:09:37.410
I mean, look, the United States
looked like it was finished.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:41.050
And imagine if Trump had actually
succeeded on January sixth

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:44.040
and the members of the
January sixth committee

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.720
in the US Congress are pretty convinced

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:48.520
that we were very close to him succeeding

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:50.661
had not Vice President Pence

00:09:50.661 --> 00:09:55.661
basically refused to block the
transfer of executive power

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:59.300
and had agreed in fact to just go ahead.

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:00.530
Pushed ahead rather than agreed to it.

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:02.700
I mean, disagreed with what
Trump was asking him to do

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:07.700
and had basically certified
the electoral votes.

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:09.320
I mean, we'd be in a
totally different place.

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:11.220
I mean Vladimir Putin
would've probably just

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:13.230
driven right into Ukraine himself,

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.810
because he then would've
seen the United States

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.490
as completely finished from
a leadership perspective,

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:19.690
because we would be no different

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:20.820
from any other country in the world

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:22.200
that had just had a coup.

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.210
How concerned are you about

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:27.290
nuclear conflict at this moment in time?

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:29.870
Well, I'm very concerned
about some one sided action

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:30.860
on the part of Putin,

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:32.440
and I think it's very
important for all of us

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:35.700
to put that into a context
and not be intimidated by it

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:37.240
because that's exactly
what he wants to do.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:39.750
Putin wants to take us
all back to the 1980s

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:42.320
and the euro missile
crisis and war scares,

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:44.510
or back to the days of
the Cuban Missile Crisis,

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:45.343
where everyone's thinking

00:10:45.343 --> 00:10:46.300
they've got to duck and cover again.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.320
Putin is a kind of a free agent maverick

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:49.570
when it comes to this.

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:52.180
He's sort of in a way
revived the Soviet era

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:54.700
instruments of nuclear missiles

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:56.190
and biological and chemical weapons,

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:57.960
he's already used chemical weapons

00:10:57.960 --> 00:10:59.270
with novichok and polonium,

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:00.760
I mean, here in the United Kingdom,

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:03.010
in assassination attempts, for example.

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:04.780
During the Syrian war, after
the Russians intervened,

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:06.590
there was lots of
chemical weapons attacks,

00:11:06.590 --> 00:11:08.460
lots of threats of all
kinds of things going

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:10.190
and just by rhetorically putting out there

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:12.800
the prospect that he might
use a nuclear weapon,

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:15.690
talking about the
chances that he might use

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:18.300
a tactical battlefield nuclear weapon,

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:19.520
he's trying to get us all scared

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:20.840
so that we back off

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:24.840
and then basically agree
to surrender Ukraine

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:26.247
or whatever else it is
that he's demanding.

00:11:26.247 --> 00:11:27.660
But do you not believe it then?

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:28.734
I suppose that's what--

00:11:28.734 --> 00:11:29.770
I believe that he's
thinking about doing it,

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:31.060
but I think that what we need to do

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:32.190
is to push back against that

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:33.910
because we need to
understand why he's doing it.

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:36.700
He's doing it because he feels
on the back foot right now.

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:39.610
He hasn't been able to
press forward with the aims

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:40.500
in the way that he wanted them

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:42.510
politically and then militarily.

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:43.800
And he's doing it because he figures

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:45.170
we're gonna get scared.

00:11:45.170 --> 00:11:46.540
So how do you do this with him?

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:47.810
I mean, you talk as if,

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:48.960
right, we need to understand him.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.801
You're giving some of the
expertise that you've got,

00:11:51.801 --> 00:11:53.400
the intelligence as well that
you will have heard about,

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.900
but how do you negotiate with Putin?

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:56.733
Well, what you have to do is

00:11:56.733 --> 00:11:58.380
get a full court press on him here,

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:00.150
because Putin wants this all to be about

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:01.940
just his discussions with Biden

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:05.470
or just his discussions
with kind of the west.

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:08.570
Because again, Ukraine is
one of the objects here

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:10.290
and the other object is rolling back NATO

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:13.000
and getting rid of the United
States in European affairs.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:15.572
But what he's doing
has global consequences

00:12:15.572 --> 00:12:18.130
and we need China, we need India,

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:20.090
we need other countries to push back.

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:21.570
Japan and South Korea get it.

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:23.180
And I mean, they've been
very much supportive,

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:24.750
but we need to get more than that.

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:26.658
We need to be able to
show the rest of the world

00:12:26.658 --> 00:12:27.930
that this has global consequences

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:29.490
and this isn't just an east west spat,

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:32.100
but it's just not possible
to keep moving forward.

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:35.140
Denying components to the
Russian military for the war,

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:38.630
not just giving more weapons to Ukraine,

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:40.550
problems of accessing the funding,

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:42.870
basically making...

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.580
Heading him off at every pass,

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:47.340
but diplomatically, if
he's hearing it from

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:48.710
not just the NATO countries,

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:51.470
not just the west, not just
the sort of usual suspects,

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:52.810
but quietly from others saying,

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:55.110
look, what you're doing here

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:57.080
is having terrible
consequences for us as well,

00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:58.730
famine in Africa,

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:00.910
kind of really putting on edge

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:03.939
security elsewhere in the world,

00:13:03.939 --> 00:13:06.003
that's when we might have
a chance of pushing this

00:13:06.003 --> 00:13:07.240
in a different direction.

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:08.527
I mean, I suppose what

00:13:08.527 --> 00:13:09.360
you're talking about there is leadership

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:10.193
and who's going to be doing that,

00:13:10.193 --> 00:13:11.026
and if they're doing that

00:13:11.026 --> 00:13:13.080
and you have worked for three presidents,

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:16.970
but only known by one as Russia bitch?

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:18.460
Were you actually known as that?

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:19.293
Apparently.

00:13:19.293 --> 00:13:20.780
I mean, I didn't know that in real time,

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:23.030
I learned that later from
an American journalist

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:26.410
who was doing some interviews
for a piece afterwards.

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:27.243
And this was how you--

00:13:27.243 --> 00:13:28.230
I was not surprised .

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:30.220
Were referred to by Donald Trump?

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:32.144
Well, by some of the people around him.

00:13:32.144 --> 00:13:34.040
He himself never said that.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:34.873
I don't think so.

00:13:34.873 --> 00:13:38.280
I think that gives a window
though into the climate

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:39.640
within which you were working,

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:41.990
especially as an expert
on Russia and as a woman.

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:42.823
Yeah, very much so.

00:13:42.823 --> 00:13:45.172
It's just that kind of casual misogyny,

00:13:45.172 --> 00:13:46.730
who does she think she is,

00:13:46.730 --> 00:13:49.160
that woman who works on Russia?

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:50.050
It's kind of obvious.

00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:51.710
I mean, I wasn't the
slightest bit surprised

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:52.734
when I heard that.

00:13:52.734 --> 00:13:53.710
I mean, I was a bit surprised

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:55.780
that I felt the particular
person it was attributed to

00:13:55.780 --> 00:13:57.523
had even noticed who I was.

00:13:57.523 --> 00:13:59.760
So I thought, oh, well, I
guess they did know who I was.

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:01.933
I wonder because of
what you're saying here,

00:14:01.933 --> 00:14:05.450
were you actually listened
to by Donald Trump?

00:14:05.450 --> 00:14:07.060
I don't think he listened to anybody,

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:09.550
but apart from people who we saw as peers.

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:13.230
I mean, that's why we saw in
weirdness in real time of him

00:14:13.230 --> 00:14:15.350
with Putin, talking about Putin
being strong and powerful,

00:14:15.350 --> 00:14:16.190
and he was listening to Putin

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:18.314
over his intelligence chiefs for example,

00:14:18.314 --> 00:14:20.788
at the summit in Helsinki and

00:14:20.788 --> 00:14:22.370
that dreadful press conferences.

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:24.710
We knew that he listened
to people on Fox News

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:28.120
and on other media outlets.

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:31.860
Many of the people, including
ambassador Bolton and others

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:33.800
would try to get on the Sunday shows

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:35.090
because they knew that
he would be watching.

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:36.960
They were more likely to
have him listen to them

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:38.210
if they were on the TV screen,

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:40.720
than he would listen if
he was in the oval office.

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:43.260
And there's all that kind
of bizarre creativity

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:45.100
that had to go on to get his attention

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:47.540
because if you went in and you sat down,

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:49.190
you were just his staff.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:51.200
So he basically projected.

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:52.990
But that sounds like it's men and women,

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:54.430
or would you say it was worse for women?

00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:56.480
I think it was worse for
women, but not all women.

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:57.313
I mean, there were some women

00:14:57.313 --> 00:14:58.330
that he respected and listened to

00:14:58.330 --> 00:15:01.380
like Kellyanne Conway and
his daughter, Ivanka Trump.

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:03.410
But once a person started working for him,

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:05.070
no matter what they had been before,

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:06.550
that status disappeared.

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:07.970
I mean, he projected onto the government

00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:11.370
the same style I presume that
he had in his private business

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:13.194
when it was the Trump family enterprises

00:15:13.194 --> 00:15:15.060
and everyone was then the staff.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:16.440
So why would he listen to them?

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:17.730
I mean, in that context,

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:21.410
information and
recommendations and direction

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:22.410
trickled down from him,

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:25.530
not up if you're in an
eponymous family firm,

00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:28.560
it's your brand, your firm,
you just tell other people,

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:30.204
you know, what to do.

00:15:30.204 --> 00:15:31.445
In the case of the U.S.
government they're supposed

00:15:31.445 --> 00:15:32.850
to be much more, you know,

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.440
advisors are supposed to be advisors,

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:36.720
national security advisor
knows their stuff,

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:38.090
they're supposed to be
able to have a say in this,

00:15:38.090 --> 00:15:40.750
they're coordinating all the
way across the government.

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:41.960
And as far as Trump was concerned,

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:44.414
we were all some version of staff.

00:15:44.414 --> 00:15:46.310
If you were called Secretary of State

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:47.190
or Secretary of Defense,

00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:48.820
you were just a glorified secretary.

00:15:48.820 --> 00:15:49.653
If you were a woman,

00:15:49.653 --> 00:15:52.663
you were pretty likely to
be a regular secretary.

00:15:52.663 --> 00:15:54.600
Did you take this in your stride?

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:55.433
Did it get to you?

00:15:55.433 --> 00:15:56.900
I know you'd been on the women's march

00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:58.650
and then you accepted this job

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:00.834
because you wanted to contribute

00:16:00.834 --> 00:16:03.480
and put something back and help.

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:05.280
Yeah, I mean, I wasn't
deluded in that sense.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:08.230
I mean, what I think really did get to me

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:10.380
was just the dirty politics.

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:12.150
Because again, I'm not a partisan person,

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:13.300
I'm politically engaged,

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:14.930
politically active, but I'm not political.

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:16.920
I've never been on a political campaign.

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:19.630
And just this Game of Thrones,

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:21.800
kind of people stabbing
each other in the back,

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:24.030
I mean, yeah, it happens
in every political system,

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:25.320
but there's just the dirty corruption

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:26.980
behind the scenes as well.

00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:29.480
I did really genuinely think
that people would understand

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:30.780
on the national security front

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:32.940
that you needed to check all of that.

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:34.340
When it came to certain issues,

00:16:34.340 --> 00:16:37.020
really critical issues where
American lives were at stake

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:39.930
or the whole stability of the
globe, let's put it that way,

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:41.950
people would check themselves.

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:44.010
I was shocked to find they
didn't and that's what got to me.

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:45.500
Well, you also said that

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:47.480
being groped in the
seventies and eighties,

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:49.220
working in a golf club,

00:16:49.220 --> 00:16:51.410
had been preparation, good
preparation for working--

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:53.570
Yeah, I would've preferred
not to have that,

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:55.070
and actually, let's just be very clear,

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:56.560
nobody did that to me in the White House,

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:57.393
but you know, just--

00:16:57.393 --> 00:16:59.640
No, but the point being, as a context

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:04.640
is an extraordinary piece of
help almost that you cited

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:06.063
when working in this particular--

00:17:06.063 --> 00:17:06.920
Yeah, I mean, that was
kind of the weird thing

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:11.630
of, you know, growing up as a
girl in 1970s, 1980s Britain,

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:13.820
you know, kind of dominated by Benny Hill

00:17:13.820 --> 00:17:15.970
and raunchy postcards at the seaside

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:17.450
and just all the things
that kind of come with that.

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:19.490
Nobody even thinks about it,
just think it's very funny.

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:20.323
But if you're a young girl,

00:17:20.323 --> 00:17:21.570
it's actually not very funny,

00:17:21.570 --> 00:17:24.228
but you just learn to
have to laugh about it.

00:17:24.228 --> 00:17:26.750
This is so pre MeToo

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:29.010
that it seems like another world

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:31.850
and yet we still get all of
that misogyny and sexism.

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:36.110
There was a dress code
inside of the White House.

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:38.330
Was kind of very much Fox News,

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:40.340
if anyone's seen the film Bombshell.

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:41.460
I mean, I thought that was actually,

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:43.000
would've been good
preparation for watching

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:45.281
before I kind of went in there.

00:17:45.281 --> 00:17:46.248
And I was always aware that I had to

00:17:46.248 --> 00:17:48.850
change the way I dress
somewhat, just so I blend in

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:51.180
so that nobody would
pay too much attention

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:54.380
and one of the funniest episodes came

00:17:54.380 --> 00:17:57.130
when he thought I was a secretary

00:17:57.130 --> 00:17:59.700
and I was told by a senior colleague,

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:02.470
you know, look, just
don't worry about that,

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:04.030
he'll forget who you are,

00:18:04.030 --> 00:18:06.220
you didn't imprint, but if
you wear the same dress,

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:07.053
he'll remember you.

00:18:07.053 --> 00:18:08.380
Just make sure that every time you go in,

00:18:08.380 --> 00:18:09.240
wear a different dress.

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:10.090
So, I had to actually get

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:12.243
quite a wardrobe of different dresses.

00:18:13.132 --> 00:18:14.258
It's a theme we seem to be returning to.

00:18:14.258 --> 00:18:15.091
Yeah.

00:18:15.091 --> 00:18:17.140
It's basically like
some kind of camouflage.

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:19.120
It's like I'll wear my
jungle camouflage today

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:21.330
or my forest camouflage
or my snow camouflage.

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:23.500
It's just so that I
could just get in there,

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:25.230
try to do my job and get out again.

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:27.000
And I'm presuming this was different

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:29.170
to working under Obama?

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:30.267
Yeah.

00:18:30.267 --> 00:18:31.100
I mean, nobody ever kind of went...

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:31.933
But that was the sort of theme then

00:18:31.933 --> 00:18:33.370
when I get to the impeachment as well,

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:35.520
nobody there gave me a dress code.

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:36.880
Actually, I looked back in
some of the pictures there,

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:38.100
I thought, what the heck was I wearing?

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:38.933
But anyway.

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:42.610
I find it actually quite funny

00:18:42.610 --> 00:18:43.960
because it was almost like

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:46.690
being in some endless school play

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:47.980
where you've got the various costumes,

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:50.060
and I guess I was just
trying to be a tree.

00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:51.460
Kind of when I got into the Oval Office,

00:18:51.460 --> 00:18:53.140
just so that nothing
untoward would happen.

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:53.973
I'm now imagining you in--

00:18:53.973 --> 00:18:55.097
Yeah, I know, exactly, the tree thing.

00:18:55.097 --> 00:18:57.730
You know, the kid in the tree,
hey mum, hey dad, I'm a tree.

00:18:57.730 --> 00:18:59.830
Well, I shouldn't say in
the corner, in the oval.

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:01.213
Yeah, exactly.

00:19:01.213 --> 00:19:02.329
Potted plant, potted plant.

00:19:02.329 --> 00:19:03.710
I've not been in there myself, but--

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:04.850
There are no trees in the oval.

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:06.480
They're usually outside
in the rose garden.

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:08.280
But the whole point of
all that was, you know,

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:09.850
get in there without any incident,

00:19:09.850 --> 00:19:12.570
but really focus on engaging
with the other counterparts.

00:19:12.570 --> 00:19:15.210
I never got treated like that
by the national security team

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:16.770
or the national security advisor.

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:20.240
It was just this weird coterie around...

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:21.240
And it was weird.

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:23.251
It was like being in Alice
In Wonderland as well,

00:19:23.251 --> 00:19:24.084
and there were so many analogies.

00:19:24.084 --> 00:19:24.917
I do feel that, you know,

00:19:24.917 --> 00:19:26.370
strange things in my childhood,

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:27.620
like reading Alice in Wonderland,

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:28.650
Alice Through the Looking Glass

00:19:28.650 --> 00:19:30.490
and other things prepared me better

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:33.970
than all of the years of
fancy educations and degrees.

00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:34.803
Although it was very important

00:19:34.803 --> 00:19:36.710
to obviously know Russia
and know your stuff,

00:19:36.710 --> 00:19:39.720
but for those moments, that
wasn't the best preparation.

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:41.950
And it isn't a partisan point
from your point of view,

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:43.350
this was the administration,

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:44.690
you would've worked
for any administration,

00:19:44.690 --> 00:19:46.860
your point is you wanted
to bring your expertise,

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:49.520
but this was how you found
this particular administration.

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:50.353
Yeah.

00:19:50.353 --> 00:19:51.220
And you mentioned that education,

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:53.610
it's a key part of what you've
written about your journey

00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:56.640
from the coal house, as you
put it to the White House.

00:19:56.640 --> 00:19:59.730
And worryingly, in your
journey, which you...

00:19:59.730 --> 00:20:02.000
There are lots of bits of luck as well.

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:03.700
The particular time you were born,

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:06.250
the education that was then available.

00:20:06.250 --> 00:20:07.370
You really talk about the fact

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:09.380
that you don't think it's getting better.

00:20:09.380 --> 00:20:10.760
In fact it's getting worse,

00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:13.680
that social mobility, not just in the UK,

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:14.513
you've looked at it through

00:20:14.513 --> 00:20:17.840
the prism of the US as well, is worse.

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:18.673
It is.

00:20:18.673 --> 00:20:20.480
It's got much worse over
time, exactly right.

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:22.490
The kind of opportunities that I had,

00:20:22.490 --> 00:20:25.120
including in the UK for
basically having my education,

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:26.930
going to university paid for,

00:20:26.930 --> 00:20:28.530
I mean those have all shrunk.

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:29.960
There's just a lot less funding up there.

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:31.560
I mean, there's been expansion of places,

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:33.150
but a lot less funding for people.

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:35.460
And just that threat of enormous debt

00:20:35.460 --> 00:20:37.052
is really too much for people.

00:20:37.052 --> 00:20:39.320
Thinking and contemplating that,

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:40.970
in the United States,

00:20:40.970 --> 00:20:42.510
what you think of as
the land of opportunity,

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:43.680
anyone can make it,

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:46.280
there's only a five percent chance now.

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:48.330
Very different from what
it was in the late 1980s

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:49.510
to do what I've done

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:51.000
in terms of going from
the bottom one percent

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:52.960
to the top one percent kind of thing.

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:55.110
And everybody should have an opportunity

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:57.360
to be able to move forward in life.

00:20:57.360 --> 00:20:58.930
It doesn't have to be in elite education,

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:00.450
but to get the kind of job that they want,

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:02.520
to get the qualifications
that they need as well,

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:03.850
we need to really think about that.

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:06.330
I mean, there's been a
lot of movement forward

00:21:06.330 --> 00:21:08.220
on socioeconomic background,

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:12.649
on race and on gender, class
to some degree here in the UK

00:21:12.649 --> 00:21:13.482
and in the United States,

00:21:13.482 --> 00:21:16.090
class gets subsumed by
race in many contexts.

00:21:16.090 --> 00:21:17.860
But in terms of regional differentiation,

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:18.740
that's not the case.

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:20.730
I mean I still see a huge divide

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:22.480
in the United Kingdom
between north and south,

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:25.700
I've just been up north to see my mother

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:26.980
and family and friends,

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:29.810
and it just seems like
London is a total world apart

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:31.490
and it's the same for people

00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:32.810
in different boroughs in London.

00:21:32.810 --> 00:21:35.740
The city couldn't be more
different from Tower Hamlets

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:37.510
and things like this, for example.

00:21:37.510 --> 00:21:39.300
And in the United States,
it's very similar as well.

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:41.570
People from, what we
now call the rust belt,

00:21:41.570 --> 00:21:42.800
we used to be the old

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:45.150
industrial heartland of the United States,

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:47.830
feel that they might as
well live on another planet,

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:49.680
as have these affiliations and affinities

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:50.600
with Washington DC.

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:52.743
That's why January sixth happened.

00:21:52.743 --> 00:21:54.810
That's why people stormed the Capitol.

00:21:54.810 --> 00:21:56.930
The Capitol, the White
House is the people's house,

00:21:56.930 --> 00:21:58.250
you know supposed to
be the people's house,

00:21:58.250 --> 00:22:00.450
but the Capitol is
supposed to be their symbol

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:01.830
of representative democracy,

00:22:01.830 --> 00:22:04.490
and yet people saw it as a citadel,

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:06.420
a kind of a forbidden fortress

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:08.280
that wasn't representing them anymore,

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:09.450
and just this kind of,

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:11.003
this upsurge and this insurgency.

00:22:11.003 --> 00:22:12.695
I watched that, I thought,

00:22:12.695 --> 00:22:14.690
oh my God, look where we've got to here.

00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:17.210
That's the kind of epitome
of these kinds of problems

00:22:17.210 --> 00:22:20.320
when people feel that
they're excluded in some way,

00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:22.790
and that sense of
representation is broken down.

00:22:22.790 --> 00:22:24.260
And you talk in some detail

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:26.040
about some of the things
you think need to change

00:22:26.040 --> 00:22:27.410
at the heart of education,

00:22:27.410 --> 00:22:28.700
but also what others can do

00:22:28.700 --> 00:22:31.230
when they do have positions
of power and influence.

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:33.700
I've got to ask, what does
your mum make of your journey?

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:35.210
Well, I think she's a bit bemused by it,

00:22:35.210 --> 00:22:37.280
but she's pretty pleased at the same time.

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:38.550
But it's funny.

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:39.383
Like she took it all in her stride

00:22:39.383 --> 00:22:40.670
and I talk in the book about

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:42.140
when I was out looking for that suit

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:43.770
that I wore at the impeachment.

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:44.860
My mum's just, you know,

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:46.780
sees a sales rack and sees this suit,

00:22:46.780 --> 00:22:48.670
and she says, Fiona come over here,

00:22:48.670 --> 00:22:49.810
look, there's a suit for sale.

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:51.510
Do you need it for that
impeachment thingy?

00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:55.160
She didn't even kind of fully
connect about what it was

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:56.790
and she just thought it was a great thing

00:22:56.790 --> 00:23:01.160
that I went there and told
the truth and did my job.

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:05.764
She wasn't passing the big
historical implications of that.

00:23:05.764 --> 00:23:08.850
My mum's a very practical
person, former midwife.

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:12.330
If a baby needed delivering in
some difficult circumstances,

00:23:12.330 --> 00:23:13.500
off she would go and just do it

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:15.860
without thinking about
all the larger context.

00:23:15.860 --> 00:23:17.080
She'd just get on with it.

00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:19.740
Well, you seem to have
adopted a similar style.

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:21.290
Thank you very much for talking to me

00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:22.350
and to all of us today.

00:23:22.350 --> 00:23:24.320
Oh, thanks so much for
having me, Emma, thank you.

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:26.150
And thank you to you for being with us.

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:28.773
Until we meet again,
take care and goodbye.

